{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1542514-v5-shooting-then-melee","id":1542514,"title":"V5 shooting then melee.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-09-22T12:35:05-07:00","id":1542514,"title":"V5 shooting then melee.","content":"So I was wondering if someone could help be with a rules clarification for V5 combat, its been a while since I've played and I'm not certain I'm understanding it correctly. Say you've got a situation where Alex has a gun, maybe with a bayonet on the gun and wants to shoot Betty. Betty has an axe she hopes to make Alex's head well acquainted with and so wants to rush Alex down and smack him (they are not yet engaged). So Alex goes first and shoots at betty, betty is still in the fight and rushes Alex, they both roll melee+ dex or strength. If Alex wins does he still deal damage even if he already used his action to try shoot Betty? Or does his success simply mean he managed to fend off Betty's assault on only on the next turn now they're both engaged can he potentially deal damage in the melee fight. Does Alex's melee roll suffer any penalties for having made the ranged attack before Betty closed to melee. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13729-the-direful-spring","name":"The Direful Spring","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-23T01:50:31-07:00","id":1542526,"title":"","content":"Under normal circumstances that would resolve as two subsequent one-sided tests, against which neither player can defend unless they want to split their dice pool. That also incentivizes all-out attacking, which is totally fine and a preferable option to just potentially eating an attack with no other recourse. But as per page 125, it's better to resolve that as a two-sided Composure + Firearms versus Strength + Melee test.<br /> <br /> In either case a character can only take one potentially damage-dealing action per turn, which means the best the character with the bayonet can do is shoot and defend.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}